,title,link,text,language,family
0,Surui language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surui_language,"November 5, 2025 - Suruí (of Jiparaná), also known as Paíter or Suruí-Paíter, is a Tupian language of Brazil. The Suruí of Rondônia call themselves Paiter, which means “the true people, we ourselves"". They speak a language of the Tupi group and Monde language family.",Suruí,Tupian
1,Tupian languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tupian_language,"August 28, 2025 - Rodrigues (2007) considers the Proto-Tupian urheimat to be somewhere between the Guaporé and Aripuanã rivers, in the Madeira River basin. Much of this area corresponds to the modern-day state of Rondônia, Brazil.",Suruí,Tupian
2,Suruí do Pará language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suruí_do_Pará_language,"November 14, 2025 - The Suruí do Pará dialect of the Tupian Akwáwa language of Brazil is spoken in the Araguaia region in the state Pará and in the town of São João do Araguaia.",Suruí,Tupian
3,Suruí do Pará dialect - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suruí_do_Pará_dialect,"January 16, 2025 - The Suruí do Pará dialect of the Tupian Akwáwa language of Brazil is spoken in the Araguaia region in the state Pará and in the town of São João do Araguaia.",Suruí,Tupian
4,Tupi–Guarani languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tupi–Guarani_languages,"2 weeks ago - Tenetehara (Group IV): Akwáwa (dialects: Asuriní, Suruí do Pará, Parakanã), Avá-Canoeiro, Tapirapé, Tenetehára (dialects: Guajajara, Tembé), Turiwára",Suruí,Tupian
5,Suruí (Pará) - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suruí_(Pará),"May 8, 2026 - First prolonged contact with the modern world came in the late 1960s. The tribe was decimated by disease. In 1960, they experienced an influenza epidemic, followed by a smallpox epidemic in 1962. The Suruí fled their homeland due to attacks by the Xikrin people.",Suruí,Tupian
6,Documentation of Gavião and Suruí Languages in whistled and instrumental speech | Endangered Languages Archive,https://www.elararchive.org/dk0228/,This collection provides a linguistic ... practices in two endangered Tupian languages of Brazilian Amazon: Gavião and Suruí. These practices consist of spoken speech emulated into whistles (for distance dialogues) or sung speech adapted into musical sounds (to perform a verbal art ...,Suruí,Tupian
7,"Suruí Language (SRU) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/sru/,It belongs to the Tupian language family. ... Looking for more detailed information about Suruí?,Suruí,Tupian
8,tupian Facts For Kids | DIY.org,https://www.diy.org/article/tupian,"Tupi languages have some exciting features! 🌈For instance, they use syllable patterns that sound musical. Tupi languages often combine words to make new meanings. For example, ""añu"" (sloth) and ""i"" (water) become ""añu'i,"" meaning ""water sloth.""",Suruí,Tupian
